{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.6,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["D20", "D33", "D40"],
      "pins": ["D20.2", "D33.2", "D40.2"],
      "nets": ["/NET0086"],
      "observed": "The common-anode net /NET0086 contains only the three LED anodes (D20 pin 2, D33 pin 2, D40 pin 2) and is not connected to any supply rail or driver. Their cathodes go through current-limit resistors (R93, R52, R78) to the CM4 open-drain status outputs (Ethernet_nLED3 /NET0038, PI_nLED_Activity /NET0111, Ethernet_nLED2 /NET0200), but with the anode node floating there is no current path.",
      "expected": "For active-low indicator LEDs driven by the CM4's open-drain nLED/PI_LED pins, the LED anodes must be tied to a positive rail (e.g. +3V3, as D9 pin 2 is). The spec describes the CM4 Ethernet/Pi-activity breakout and discrete status LEDs as intended features, so these LEDs are expected to illuminate.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"24\") (name \"/NET0086\") ... (node (ref \"D20\") (pin \"2\") ...) (node (ref \"D33\") (pin \"2\") ...) (node (ref \"D40\") (pin \"2\") ...))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"1\") (name \"+3V3\") ... (node (ref \"D9\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\") ...))"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "hosts the CM4 ... breaking out GPIO, Ethernet, USB ... and an RTC"}
      ],
      "impact": "The three discrete status LEDs (D20 green, D33 green, D40 yellow), which are wired to the CM4's Ethernet link/activity and Pi-activity open-drain outputs, can never light because their common anode has no connection to a supply. The board's Ethernet/Pi status indication is dead.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The netlist shows the common-anode net /NET0086 tying together only the anodes of D20, D33 and D40 with no connection to +3V3 (or any driver), while their cathodes are pulled to the CM4's open-drain nLED/PI_LED pins; this leaves those status LEDs with no current path and permanently off, a deviation from the intended CM4 Ethernet/Pi-activity LED breakout. Other apparent oddities (reference-designator mismatches with the spec, the SM712/LTC4412/16 MHz-crystal items, the open-drain vs push-pull buffer MPN, the documented USB/GLOBAL_EN issues) are consistent with the spec or with standard practice and are not reported."
}

